# Report requirements

Soleox has three report sets: Owner Reports, Agent Reports, and Office Reports. Requirements in All Reports apply to every set.

When a new chat should follow this before editing reports, say: read `.cursor/rules/report-requirements.mdc` before making changes.

## All Reports

### Navigation

On a report page the top navigation is hidden. Hovering the top of the page slides it into view. It hides again when the pointer leaves it.

### Filters

Group, Status, Side, Primary agent, and Lead source use the multi-select control on every report. Metric stays a single-select: one measure.

Date range is a single-select dropdown in that same style.

- From and To appear only when Custom is selected. Each is typed as MM/DD/YYYY, the year stops at four digits, and a calendar button opens the date picker.
- Every other option shows its name and, in grey, the dates that name covers, as `MM/DD/YYYY - MM/DD/YYYY`. Example: Year to date 01/01/2026 - 10/09/2026.
- Show a search box when there are more than 8 presets.

- When 1 to 3 options are selected, the closed filter lists those names. When 4 or more are selected, it shows the count and filter name, such as "4 groups" or "4 lead sources".
- When every option is unchecked, the filter shows None and highlights in amber so the empty selection is visible.
- The default is every listed option. A report may pre-select a subset instead. Leaderboard by Date, Team Performance, and Agent Earnings status default to Closed and Pending, and those reports never include any other status.
- Show a search box when there are more than 8 options.
- Each option shows a right-aligned record count: transactions with that value after the other filters, not after this filter.
- Hovering an option shows ONLY over its count. ONLY keeps that value and clears the others.
- Reset restores that filter’s default for the current report.
- Select all checks every option currently listed.
- Options stay dynamic. A value is listed only when the current report view still has records for it after the other filters.
- The open list is taller when the screen has room, up to 36rem, and it stays within the viewport when the screen is shorter.

### Tables

- A table header stays fixed just below the report title and filters while that table is on screen. The stuck header stays square so the table body does not show past its corners. It scrolls away once the user has moved past the end of the table.
- Every column header sorts that table when clicked. The first click sorts ascending. Clicking the same header again sorts descending. The active header shows the direction. Each table sorts on its own. Total and grand total rows stay at the bottom. A table keeps its original order until a header is clicked.
- When a table has a # index column, that column is right-aligned, header and cells.
- Keep each report table in a `report-table` wrapper so wide tables can scroll sideways. Pin the header from the page scroll while the table crosses the title and filters. Do not use CSS `top` sticky for that pin: horizontal scrolling makes the table its own scroll container, and the header then sticks inside the table. Do not put `overflow: hidden` on a table wrapper.

## Owner Reports

The reports menu button shows only the current report’s number. Hovering it opens the full menu. A click outside that menu closes it, does not activate whatever is underneath, and the button returns to the number.

Leaderboard by Date ranks closed and pending transactions. The last words of the title choose whether the report range applies to `closing_date` or `acceptance_date`. `closing_date` is the default. Those words, and the date filter label, use the field label saved for that key in the transaction schema. A team that names `acceptance_date` Pending Date sees Pending Date in the title and Pending Date Range on the filter. The same rule applies to `closing_date`. Its metric lists every enabled transaction field whose control type is a dropdown (Select Dropdown) or a list picker (Agent Picker), except `gci_type`, `transaction_status`, and `finance_status`. The selected field is what each row groups by. Sales volume and units stay the two boards. Lead Source is the default metric.

Agent Earnings and Entity Payments are extra metric choices. Each payment record is joined to its transaction, so the report keeps that transaction’s status and the selected date. Archived payments stay out. The left board sums the payment amount received by each agent or entity. The right board counts those payment records. While one of those metrics is selected, an Agent or Entity dropdown lists the payees in the current view. Unchecking a name removes that payee from both boards. Its counts are payment records after the other filters. Every metric except Entity Payments has an Exclude Team Lead(s) checkbox. Checked, it removes transactions whose primary agent has `is_team_lead` true from both boards and from the filters. Agent Earnings instead removes those agents as payees from the Agent dropdown and both boards. Entity Payments does not show the checkbox.

The metric is the first words of the report title. The selected date field’s label is the last words. Both use the title font, a different color, and no border or background. Clicking either opens its list. The date filter on this report is labeled with that same field label plus Range.

The % Δ column header has an info note. Hover or click it to read that the column is how much each value of the selected metric changed from the same dates in the prior year, and that a dash means the prior year was zero.

Pending scorecards reserve the same space as the Total and Closed comparison row, so the name and amount sit on the same line. The Total label is white in dark mode.

Agent Earnings lists closed and pending transactions by closing date. An Exclude Team Lead(s) checkbox starts unchecked. Checked, it removes those agents from the Agent dropdown, the scorecards, the pivot, and the transaction table. An Agent dropdown lists the payees in the current view. Unchecking a name removes that payee from the scorecards, the pivot, and the transaction table. Its counts are payment records after the other filters. Total, Closed, and Pending scorecards sit above the pivot and sum those earnings. Total and Closed show the change from the same dates last year. Pending reserves that comparison space. Clicking a scorecard filters the pivot and the transaction table to that status, and clicking it again restores every status. The scorecard amounts stay the full totals. Total keeps the plain amount color, Closed uses emerald for its name and amount, and Pending uses that same emerald, lighter in light mode and darker in dark mode, for its name and amount. The Total label is white in dark mode. Each cell is the sum of agent payment amounts on those transactions, the same dollars as the Agent Earnings metric on Leaderboard by Date. Each amount has a thin bright green bar with round edges directly under it. The bar’s right end lines up with the number, and its length runs to the left. A month bar is that amount against the largest amount in the same month. A row total is that agent against the largest agent total. A total-row month is that month against the largest month. Columns are the months in the selected range, or days when the range is 62 days or shorter. Rows are the agents who received a payment, sorted by the Total column with the highest first. A # column on the left shows each agent’s place in that order, and it stays with the agent when another column is sorted. The transaction table has a # column to the left of Agent for the order of those rows, and that number stays with the row when another column is sorted. Its total row stays pinned to the bottom of the table while the rows scroll. The far-right column is that agent’s total. It has a light left edge and a slightly different background so it reads as the total. A total row stays pinned to the bottom of the pivot while the agent rows scroll. A dash means that agent received nothing in that period. Clicking an amount, a row total, a column total, or the grand total compresses the pivot to that row and shows the transaction table with the payments inside that figure and the amount that agent received on each transaction. Until an amount is clicked, the transaction table stays hidden. Clicking the selected amount again restores every pivot row and hides the transaction table. The caption reads like Robert Ito's earnings: August 2026. A month uses its full name and year. A day uses the full date and year. A total uses the Closing Date Range name and dates, such as Year to date (01/01/2026 - 10/11/2026). The caption ends with Click the same amount again to show all agents. The pivot, while it shows every agent, and the transaction table each stay within the screen height and scroll inside that height. Both tables use the Owner Summary month table’s dark headers, and their rows alternate, and in light mode the even rows use a slightly darker grey. The Owner Summary month table and both Agent Earnings tables have no horizontal lines between rows.

Team Performance ranks closed and pending transactions. An Exclude Team Lead(s) checkbox starts unchecked. Checked, it removes transactions whose primary agent has `is_team_lead` true from the scorecards, charts, and the other filters. The last words of the title choose whether the report range applies to `closing_date` or `acceptance_date`. `closing_date` is the default. Those words, and the date filter label, use the field label saved for that key, the same way Leaderboard by Date does. Five panels show Sales volume (sum of sales price), Gross commission income (sum of GCI), Units (transaction count), Gross commission percentage (GCI divided by sales price), and Sales price (sales price divided by units) below Units. A sixth panel, Team Commission, sits beside Units. It is a line chart of the sum of `final_amount` on each transaction’s Agent Split commission lines. `final_amount` is the dollar the waterfall shows for that line. An info note on the heading says Team Commission is the commission left after off-the-top deductions and pre-splits are subtracted from Total Commission. Sales price and Gross commission percentage sit together on the bottom row. Those two panels count only transactions whose side is Buyer or Seller. When the Side filter leaves both of those sides unchecked, the scorecards and charts are replaced by a note that the two sections are specific to Buyer and Seller transactions only. Each line is the monthly value against the same months last year, using the selected date. The line is a smooth curve, and it rounds where it levels off. This period has a light fill down to the axis that fades from the line color to transparent at the bottom. The current period line has a light shine along its peaks. A Cumulative control, off until it is turned on, shows a grey X while off and a check while on, and makes the line charts a running total through the period. The bar charts stay monthly averages. Gross commission percentage and Sales price are vertical bar charts with rounded tops. The current period is a filled bar. Each month has two bars side by side. The current period bar is filled in the chart color. The previous year bar is filled grey. The bars are wide. The previous year line and bar are grey. In light mode that grey is twice as dark as the previous light grey. Legends sit centered under every line chart and bar chart and read Current period. Average: amount and Previous year. Average: amount, with the amount standing out. A horizontal line of long dashes in that series color marks each of those averages on every line chart and bar chart. Hovering a month shows the metric name, the abbreviated date for this period and the prior year with each amount on the right, and the difference with its percent. The popup stays wide enough for those four lines. Total and Closed show the change from last year. Clicking Total, Closed, or Pending highlights that status on every panel and filters the charts to it. Clicking the selected status again restores the total. The scorecard amounts stay the full totals. Total keeps the plain amount color, Closed uses the section color for its name and amount, and Pending uses that same section color, lighter in light mode and darker in dark mode, for its name and amount. The Total label is white in dark mode.

